{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Logging landed; 20 live runs; no failure; cleanup confirmed (session rebar-session-3d705fec, 2026-11-17 PST)\n\n### Change\n- Gerrit 3419 merged at `05c6414358fdc8edc70408a6d5612ef23ba0a5ff` (2026-11-17 18:10:57 PST), LLM-Review +1, Verified +1, 0 unresolved. `make verify` exited 0 before the push (21359 passed in the default tier, 226 passed in the integration tier).\n- `_search_outcome` records each search as `ok N hits`, `error HTTP <code>: <reason> - <body start>` or `error <ExcType>: <message>`. `_poll_until_visible(..., attempts=)` appends `t=<s>s key: <note>; summary: <note>` per attempt, plus a final key line. The probe's assertion message lists every attempt, and the probe prints `delete-permission probe created <KEY>`.\n- The probe issue's summary is now `[rebar probe - safe to delete] DELETE-ME-d01e delete-permission probe`, and its description asks for it to be deleted if it is still present. A read-only JQL check before the change confirmed that Jira accepts the bracketed summary in `summary ~ \"...\"` (HTTP 200).\n\n### Live runs\nThese are 20 sequential runs of `pytest -m live tests/integration/test_reconcile_live_e2e.py::test_delete_permission_probe` from a worktree at Gerrit main `05c6414358f`. All 20 passed, in 5.8-8.9 s each. No failure was captured.\nCreated keys: REB-7252, REB-7253, REB-7254, REB-7255, REB-7256, REB-7257, REB-7258, REB-7259, REB-7260, REB-7261, REB-7262, REB-7263, REB-7264, REB-7265, REB-7266, REB-7267, REB-7268, REB-7269, REB-7270, REB-7271.\nREB-7251 falls inside that range but is unrelated. It is \"Streamed reclaim collapse fails on Windows...\" and was created 2026-11-17 11:32 PST. It was not touched.\n\n### Cleanup: Jira status from GET /rest/api/3/issue/<key>; rebar status from a git grep of the fetched tickets branch (4adcdcefda4) plus MCP search\n- REB-7252 through REB-7271 (20 keys): Jira 404 (the probe deleted each one). Rebar has no copy: no event or binding mentions any of them.\n- REB-7248, REB-7249, REB-7250: Jira 404. Rebar has no copy.\n- REB-6995: Jira 404. Rebar has no copy.\n- REB-1981: this was an additional leaked probe issue. Its summary is the old `DELETE-ME-d01e delete-permission probe`, and it was created 2026-08-01 03:38 PDT. It was the only issue matching `summary ~ \"DELETE-ME-d01e\"`. I deleted it from Jira (`delete_issue` returned `deleted`; a readback returns 404). Its rebar copy `jira-reb-1936` was closed. Archive accepts only open tickets, so I reopened the copy and then archived it; it now reads `status: archived`. Its binding row in `.bridge_state` is reconciler-managed state, and the 404-grace retirement handles it. A bound issue that returns 404 is never re-created.\n- JQL `summary ~ \"rebar probe - safe to delete\"` and a tickets-branch grep for the prefix found no surviving issue and no imported copy.\n\n### Status\nAC 1-5 are met. AC 6 (cause identified and fixed) is NOT met: the 20 isolated runs did not reproduce the failure. The original failure occurred inside the full `-m live` group under load 25-40, which these isolated runs did not recreate. Following the operator's decision, this bug stays OPEN. The next `-m live` failure will now name its cause in the assertion message: index lag shows as `ok 0 hits`, and a rejected search shows as `error HTTP <code>`. I am not ticking the AC boxes, so the signed plan material stays unchanged."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1794968612349616009,"uuid":"efe94d51-444c-4518-8525-b9932ee37325"}